1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,940
 Hello, and welcome back to the Articulate Journal. Articulate is a public engineering journal documenting the design and implementation of an AI-native architecture platform. Every episode explores another architectural question, and together they'll explain how the system evolves. In the previous video episode, I talked about the characteristics every production system needs, whether it's AI-native or not. Qualities like reliability,

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,000
 scalability, security, and observability don't disappear, just because we introduce AI. They become even more important. In this episode, I'd like to turn our attention to the conceptual data model that will hold architectural knowledge. Before I can build AI that helps architects understand architecture, I first need to understand a question. What is architectural knowledge? At first, I thought the answer would be obvious. Architecture is made up of diagrams, documents, architecture, and architecture.

3
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:08,000
 Performance, architecture decision records, source code, all the things we create throughout the lifetime of a system.

4
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 But the more I thought about it, the more I realized that these things aren't knowledge at all, just information, useful certainly, but information.

5
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 Imagine joining a new organization as an architect, and your first day you'll give an access to an architecture repository.

6
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:36,000
 Thousands of diagrams, hundreds of pages of documentation, collection of ADRs, wikis, slide decks, maybe recording from the previous design review.

7
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 Now you have a huge amount of information. But do you understand the architecture? Probably not.

8
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 Understanding doesn't come from reading one document. It comes from gradually connecting all those pieces together.

9
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:56,000
 You begin to see how the systems relate to one another. Why particular decisions were made?

10
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:03,000
 Which principles shape the architecture? Where the risks are? Which assumptions are still valid?

11
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:07,000
 And which parts of the architecture have evolved over time?

12
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 That's when information starts to become knowledge.

13
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:24,000
 That distinction became one of the most important ideas in the design of Articulate. Because if I simply build a platform that just stores documents, then all I've really created is just another document repository.

14
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 What I want to do is to model the understanding that emerges from those documents. Not just how architecture says, but what the architecture means.

15
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:45,000
 That becomes the foundation of everything that follows in this episode. Once I start thinking about the architectural knowledge, another question naturally followed.

16
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:56,000
 What information actually exists in an architecture? Again, this seemed obvious. We have systems, applications, services, databases and infrastructure.

17
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:07,000
 Those are certainly part of the architecture, but they're only part of the picture. Architecture also includes the principles that guide decisions.

18
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:13,000
 The characteristics that the system needs to exhibit. The decisions that have been made over time.

19
00:03:13,000 --> 00:03:23,000
 The relationships between different parts of the estate. The assumptions people are working with and the constraints they have to operate within.

20
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 And the evidence that supports those decisions. Taken together, that's a huge amount of information.

21
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:42,000
 Some of it is highly structured. Some of it is buried inside documents. Some of it only exists because someone remembers where a decision was made three years ago.

22
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 The challenge isn't finding places to store that information. The challenge is bringing it together in a way that allows us to reason about it.

23
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:59,000
 That brought me to yet another important distinction. The difference between information and knowledge.

24
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:06,000
 Suppose articulate knows that a payment services uses Postgres SQL.

25
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:15,000
 On its own, that's simply a piece of information. Now suppose it also knows that the service processes financial transactions.

26
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:25,000
 That high availability is a characteristic. That an architecture decision record explains why Postgres SQL was selected.

27
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:36,000
 That another proposal to move to a different database was rejected. And that recent operational issues suggest that the original assumptions may longer hold.

28
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:45,000
 Individually, all of these are just pieces of information. But once they're connected together, something interesting happens.

29
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,000
 The platform doesn't just know isolated facts. It begins to understand the context surrounding them.

30
00:04:52,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 That's much closer to what I think as knowledge. After thinking about this for quite a while, I eventually arrived at a very simple definition.

31
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:13,000
 Knowledge is information combined with thinking. That almost sounds too simple. But that single idea shaped how I thought about the entire platform.

32
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:20,000
 The goal isn't to collect as much information as possible. The goal is to build a model that captures understanding.

33
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
 Because that's what architects actually work with every day.

34
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:32,000
 Once they reach that conclusion, that knowledge is information combined with thinking, another question emerged.

35
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:43,000
 How should that knowledge actually be represented? The more I explored the problem, the more I realized that almost everything we know about architecture can be expressed as a claim.

36
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:56,000
 A claim is simply an assertion about the architecture. For example, order service uses Postgres SQL. Checkout capability requires high availability.

37
00:05:56,000 --> 00:06:01,000
 Product API is consumed by three downstream search systems.

38
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:15,000
 Architectural decision number 16 was made to improve resilience. Each of those statements tells us something about the architecture. But on their own, they just isolated assertions.

39
00:06:15,000 --> 00:06:20,000
 What makes them useful is the context that surrounds them.

40
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:30,000
 Where did this claim come from? Was it discovered in source code? Was it taken from an architecture decision record? Was it suggested during a conversation with an architect?

41
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:41,000
 Or generated by AI during discovery? How confident are we that that's correct and what evidence supports it? Has it always been true?

42
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:48,000
 Or has the architecture evolved over time? Those questions become just as important as the claim itself.

43
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:57,000
 Rather than storing disconnected facts, the knowledge model stores claims together with the information needed to understand and evaluate them.

44
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:04,000
 That allows the model to grow over time. New evidence can strengthen existing claims.

45
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:11,000
 Conflicting evidence can challenge it. And as the architecture evolves, the understanding can evolve with it.

46
00:07:11,000 --> 00:07:15,000
 I think that's a much better reflection of how architects work in the real world.

47
00:07:15,000 --> 00:07:21,000
 We rarely have perfect knowledge. Instead, we gradually build confidence as more information becomes available.

48
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:31,000
 The knowledge model isn't trying to eliminate uncertainty. It's trying to make uncertainty explicit, and I think that's much more honest representation of architecture.

49
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:39,000
 By this point, I had the beginnings of a model. I had the architecture information, and I had the relationships between the information.

50
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:45,000
 I had claims that represented what the system currently understood about the architecture.

51
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:54,000
 Something still didn't feel quite right. A collection of connected claims is certainly more useful than a collection of disconnected documents.

52
00:07:54,000 --> 00:08:00,000
 But it still isn't knowledge. Knowledge comes from interpreting those claims.

53
00:08:00,000 --> 00:08:09,000
 It comes from understanding how they relate to one another, and how they support and contradict each other, and how confidence we are in them.

54
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
 And what they actually mean in the context of the wider architecture.

55
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:24,000
 For example, imagine articulate discovers that a service has suffered several operational incidents.

56
00:08:24,000 --> 00:08:32,000
 On its own, that's just another claim. But it also understands that the service supports a business critical capability.

57
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:41,000
 That reliability is one of those architectural principles that an earlier ADR accepted a trade-off in favor of performance.

58
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:45,000
 And that the current maturity of that area is relatively low.

59
00:08:45,000 --> 00:08:50,000
 Then the platform can begin to reason about the significance of those observations.

60
00:08:50,000 --> 00:08:55,000
 It isn't simply retrieving information, it's building understanding.

61
00:08:55,000 --> 00:09:01,000
 That to me is the difference between an information model and a knowledge model.

62
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
 The purpose of the knowledge model isn't to replace architectural judgment.

63
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:15,000
 It's to provide enough connected understanding that both humans and AI can reason about the architecture more effectively.

64
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:21,000
 That's the point where architecture starts to become more than just a collection of documents.

65
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:28,000
 It becomes a body of knowledge that can grow, evolve, and support better architectural decisions over time.

66
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:35,000
 By this point, I felt like I had enough understanding to describe what the knowledge model actually is.

67
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:40,000
 It's important to remember that I'm not thinking about databases or implementation.

68
00:09:40,000 --> 00:09:43,000
 There's one piece of terminology that I'd like to explain.

69
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:50,000
 Throughout this episode, I've deliberately referred to it as a knowledge model and not as a knowledge graph.

70
00:09:50,000 --> 00:09:52,000
 That choice was intentional.

71
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:02,000
 It may well turn out that a graph is the best way to implement it, but at this stage, I didn't want to introduce an implementation bias into the architectural thinking.

72
00:10:02,000 --> 00:10:07,000
 Right now, I'm trying to understand the concepts that it needed to represent.

73
00:10:07,000 --> 00:10:12,000
 The structure of the knowledge, the relationships, and the behaviour.

74
00:10:12,000 --> 00:10:19,000
 Only once I understand a conceptual model do I want to start thinking about how it should be implemented.

75
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:23,000
 That implementation might involve a graph database.

76
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:27,000
 It might evolve to use several different technologies working together.

77
00:10:27,000 --> 00:10:31,000
 I simply just don't know yet, and that's perfectly fine.

78
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:34,000
 I'm thinking about the conceptual model.

79
00:10:34,000 --> 00:10:40,000
 The knowledge model is the way Articulate represents architectural understanding.

80
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:45,000
 It brings together the architectural concepts we've explored throughout this episode.

81
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:49,000
 Structure, characteristics, principles, and decisions.

82
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
 And the claims that connect them.

83
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:57,000
 Each claim captures something the platform currently understands about the architecture.

84
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:03,000
 Together, those claims form a connected body of knowledge rather than a collection of disconnected facts.

85
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:10,000
 What I particularly like about this approach is that the model can evolve as new information is discovered, as decisions are made,

86
00:11:10,000 --> 00:11:15,000
 as the system changes, and as more evidence becomes available.

87
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:18,000
 The model evolves alongside the architecture itself.

88
00:11:18,000 --> 00:11:22,000
 Nothing needs to be treated as permanently fixed.

89
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:26,000
 The understanding simply becomes richer over time.

90
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:33,000
 That also means that the knowledge model becomes much more than somewhere just to store architecture information.

91
00:11:33,000 --> 00:11:38,000
 It becomes the foundation that every other capability in Articulate builds upon.

92
00:11:38,000 --> 00:11:41,000
 Discovery adds to it.

93
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
 Conversations retrieve knowledge from it.

94
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:46,000
 Impact analysis reasons over it.

95
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:49,000
 Architecture decisions become part of it.

96
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:53,000
 Future AI agents use it as context for their own reasoning.

97
00:11:53,000 --> 00:12:00,000
 Instead of every capability maintaining its own understanding of the architecture, they all work from the same shared model.

98
00:12:00,000 --> 00:12:04,000
 And for me, that's one of the most important architectural ideas in Articulate.

99
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:09,000
 The knowledge model becomes the system's shared understanding of the architecture.

100
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:17,000
 Everything else is simply another way of contributing to that understanding, reasoning, and presenting it to the user.

101
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:21,000
 Once I have the knowledge model, another question naturally followed.

102
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:26,000
 How does the rest of the platform interpret or interact with it?

103
00:12:26,000 --> 00:12:29,000
 My answer was to introduce a knowledge API.

104
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:33,000
 The purpose of the API isn't simply to read and write data.

105
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:39,000
 Its purpose is to provide a consistent way for every part of the platform to work with architectural knowledge.

106
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
 Discovery can add new claims.

107
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:45,000
 Conversations can retrieve architectural context.

108
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:52,000
 Reasoning services can explore the relationships and impact analysis can understand dependencies.

109
00:12:52,000 --> 00:12:58,000
 Future agents can query the model without needing to know how it's stored internally.

110
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:00,000
 And that separation is important.

111
00:13:00,000 --> 00:13:08,000
 The rest of the platform shouldn't care whether the knowledge model was backed by a graph database, document database, or relational database, or something else entirely.

112
00:13:08,000 --> 00:13:11,000
 Those are implementation details.

113
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:16,000
 The capabilities of the platform interact with the knowledge API.

114
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:20,000
 And the knowledge API interacts with the knowledge model.

115
00:13:20,000 --> 00:13:26,000
 That gives me the freedom to evolve and the implementation without changing the architecture built on top of it.

116
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:32,000
 More importantly, it establishes the knowledge model as a first-class architectural capability.

117
00:13:32,000 --> 00:13:36,000
 It isn't just another database hidden behind a service.

118
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:44,000
 It's a shared understanding of the architecture exposed through a well-defined interface that other capabilities can build upon.

119
00:13:44,000 --> 00:13:49,000
 As the platform evolves, I expect the API to evolve as well.

120
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:52,000
 New ways of querying knowledge will emerge.

121
00:13:52,000 --> 00:13:55,000
 New reasoning capabilities will be introduced.

122
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
 The model itself will become richer.

123
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
 But the architectural idea remains the same.

124
00:14:00,000 --> 00:14:03,000
 There is one knowledge model.

125
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:08,000
 And there is one consistent way for the rest of the platform to understand and contribute to it.

126
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:13,000
 Stepping back from the details of the knowledge model, I realize something interesting.

127
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:20,000
 What I'm really trying to do is to build not just another architecture repository, and it isn't another modeling tool.

128
00:14:20,000 --> 00:14:26,000
 And it certainly isn't another AI chatbot that happens to know something about software architecture.

129
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:30,000
 What I'm exploring is something that I'd like to think of as architectural intelligence.

130
00:14:30,000 --> 00:14:39,000
 The ability for a system to understand architecture in much the same way as an architect gradually builds understanding over time.

131
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:49,000
 Not by memorizing the documents or by indexing diagrams, but by connecting information, evaluating evidence, understanding relationships and the reasoning about change.

132
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:53,000
 That's why the knowledge model is so important.

133
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:59,000
 Without it, AI is simply generating responses to whatever information just happens to be available.

134
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:04,000
 With it, the platform has a shared understanding that it can reason over.

135
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:08,000
 That doesn't mean that AI replaces the architect far from it.

136
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:11,000
 The architect still makes the decisions.

137
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:14,000
 The architect still applies judgment.

138
00:15:14,000 --> 00:15:17,000
 The architect is still accountable for the outcome.

139
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:20,000
 The role of AI is to help build understanding.

140
00:15:20,000 --> 00:15:24,000
 To connect the ideas that might otherwise remain disconnected.

141
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:30,000
 To service the relationships and to identify contradictions and to explain the consequences of change.

142
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:37,000
 And to help architects navigate increasingly complex systems.

143
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:42,000
 Whether that vision is achievable is something this journal will continue to explore.

144
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:54,000
 But I do believe it starts with the knowledge model, because before an AI can help us reason about architecture, it first needs a meaningful representation of what the architecture is.

145
00:15:54,000 --> 00:16:00,000
 This episode has been less about technology, more about the understanding of the problem I'm trying to solve.

146
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:07,000
 Before I can build an AI native architecture platform, I first need a way of representing the architectural knowledge.

147
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:17,000
 That journey led me from architecture information to connected claims to a knowledge model and ultimately to the idea of architecture intelligence.

148
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:24,000
 Now that I have a conceptual model for the architectural knowledge, another question naturally follows.

149
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:27,000
 How does AI actually reason over it?

150
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:31,000
 Some questions might be answered throughout semantic search.

151
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:37,000
 Others might require traversing relationships and some might need structured queries.

152
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:41,000
 And some may require several different approaches working together.

153
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:49,000
 In the next episode, we'll begin exploring how AI retrieves, reasons over and makes use of the knowledge model.

154
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:52,000
 Because building a model is only half of the challenge.

155
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:59,000
 The real value comes from helping both humans and AI use that knowledge to make better architectural decisions.

156
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,440
 I'll see you on the next episode.

